{{inh+|fr|fro|hom}}, {{m|fro|om}} (nominative form), from {{inh|fr|la|homō||human being}} (compare {{m|fr|homme}} from the Old French oblique form {{m|fro|home}}, from the Latin accusative form {{m|la|hominem}}). Its pronominal use is of {{cog|gem}} origin. Compare {{cog|ang|man||one, they, people}}, reduced form of {{cog|ang|mann||person}}; {{cog|ca|hom}}; {{cog|de|man||one, they, people}}; {{cog|nl|men||one, they, people}}. In the second sense, meaning "we", also compare the development {{noncog|ms|kita orang||we (incl.) + person}} and also dialectal ''kitorang'', ''kitong'', ''torang''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{fr-IPA|+|qual2=before a vowel|o.n‿}}
* {{audio|fr|Fr-on.ogg}}
* {{homophones|fr|ont}}

===Pronoun===
{{fr-pron|type=personal}}

# {{lb|fr|indefinite}} [[one]], [[people]], [[you]], [[someone]] {{gloss|an unspecified individual}}
#: {{syn|fr|[[quelqu'un]] {{q|in some contexts}}|[[l'on]] {{q|formal}}}}
#* '''2003''', Natasha St. Pier, ''L’instant d’après'' (album), ''Quand on cherche l’amour'' (song)
#*: {{quote|fr|Quand '''on''' cherche l'amour…|When '''one''' searches for love…}}
#: {{uxi|fr|'''On''' ne peut pas pêcher ici|'''You''' can’t fish here}}
# {{lb|fr|personal|informal}} [[we]]
#: {{syn|fr|[[nous]] {{q|in some contexts}}}}
#* {{quote-text|fr|year=2021|author=Zaz|title=Tout là-haut
|passage='''On''' oublie nos certitudes|t='''We''' forget our certainties}}
#: {{uxi|fr|'''On''' s’est amusés.|'''We''' had fun.}}

====Usage notes====
* In informal and standard conversational French, {{m|fr|on}} has almost completely replaced the pronoun {{m|fr|nous||we}} to indicate that a sentence or clause has a  first-person plural as its subject. However, {{m|fr||nous}} is still favored in formal writing and speech, and is still used colloquially as a disjunctive reinforcing nominative {{m|fr||on}}, as well as to indicate direct and indirect objects. It may be used for reflexive objects, but as this is potentially ambiguous, these are also indicated with the reflexive pronoun {{m|fr|se}} — especially with reinforcement from disjunctive {{m|fr|nous}}, which clarifies that the speaker means "we" and not "one," i.e. a generalized indefinite subject. This clarification can also be achieved by the use of {{m|fr|tous}}.
*: {{uxi|fr|On est toujours là.|We're still here.}}
*: {{uxi|fr|Nous, on s'y fait.|We get used to it.}}
*: {{uxi|fr|On connait tous la chanson qu'elle chante.|We all know which song she is singing.}}
*: {{uxi|fr|Nous, on l'a tous vu.|We all saw it.}}
* The verb is always conjugated in the third-person singular, but if the pronoun refers to a first-person plural, the attribute agrees in gender and number.
*: {{uxi|fr|On est venu ici.|One came here.}}
*: {{uxi|fr|On y est allé'''s''' / allé'''es'''.|We went there.}}
*: {{uxi|fr|On est prêt'''s''' / prêt'''es'''.|We are ready.}}
* The variant {{m|fr|l'on}} is used in more formal or literary contexts. Some use it especially after {{m|fr|que}} ({{m|fr||que l'on}}) to avoid the contraction {{m|fr|qu'on}}, which is homophonous with the vulgar word {{m|fr|con}}.

